<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wulfred</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 832</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Wulfred of Canterbury</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wulfred was Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> from 805 to 832. A reformer, he required the clergy of his cathedral to live a common life and sought to bring monasteries under episcopal control. His long dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coenwulf-of-mercia">Coenwulf of Mercia</a> over the ownership of Kentish minsters led to his exclusion from office for several years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wulfred.jpg" alt="Wulfred" width="320" height="197" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: St. Albans District Council, Julian Watters, 2014-05-01 16:07:14 · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Early-Medieval_coin,_penny_of_Wulfred,_Archbishop_of_Canterbury_(FindID_614314).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
